    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 061217\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n517 AM MST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Major waterways such as the Salt and Gila rivers will continue \n  to experience high volume flows through at least the weekend due\n  to dam releases.\n\n- With the exception of a slight chance for showers and\n  thunderstorms over parts of south-central Arizona today, dry\n  weather will be common through at least the middle of next week.\n\n- Temperatures will warm very near daily normals through the \n  middle of next week. \n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Sunday/...\nEarly morning objective analysis depicts a more progressive flow \npattern across the Conus with elongated troughing over the Great \nLakes/Ohio River valley, as well as extending from the northern \nRockies into the lower Colorado River valley. In fact, WV imagery \nhighlights a very distinct vorticity center descending into \nsouthern Nevada which will become a major player in the local \nforecast over the next 24 hours. Trends in both global and high \nresolution modeling point towards more rapid deepening and forced \nascent as this feature propagates into south-central Arizona by \nthis afternoon. Recent sfc dewpoints suggest higher low level \nmoisture content than models have previously forecast, and with \ndiurnal mixing and upslope flow, mixing ratios 6-7 g/kg ascending \nand saturating near the H7 layer seems attainable. As the \nvorticity center intensifies and becomes a closed circulation, \nsynoptic ascent will also increase further supporting the \nformation of scattered showers from Phoenix north and east. While \nmidlevel lapse rates are not particularly steep, the introduction \nof modest cooling aloft and moisture intrusion at the top of the \nboundary layer may tap 100 J/kg MLCape leading to a few isolated \nthunderstorms. Most recent initialized NBM POPs were higher than \nprevious iterations, however have raised and expanded these values\nsomewhat based on the various HRRR and REFS trends. \n\nIn the immediate wake of this vorticty center and on the western \nperiphery of the closed circulation, higher momentum northerly \nwinds will surge down the lower Colorado River valley this \nafternoon through Saturday morning. Winds speeds within the H9-H8 \nmixing depth do not appear any stronger than 20-30kt, and high \nresolution models forecast gusts over 30 mph only across ridge \ntops and 20-30 mph at lower elevation population centers resulting\nin minimal impacts. As the cutoff vorticity center travels into \nnorthern Mexico over the weekend, sharp ridging over the Great \nBasin will force strong subsidence and sfc pressure rises. This \nclassic cold season pattern will shift enhanced nocturnal/morning \neasterly winds into south- central Arizona further drying the \nenvironment above the H9 layer with total column PWATs falling \nwell below 0.50\". Otherwise, notable H5 height rises behind the \nexiting wave with readings 582-585dm will support temperatures \nwarming back very near daily normals. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Monday through Thursday/... \nWith northern stream jet energy retreating towards the Canadian \nborder under broad ridging, several weak low amplitude features \nwill align in the southern stream over northern Mexico during the \nfirst half of the week before large scale hemispheric pattern \namplification becomes a dominating feature. Predominant anti- \ncyclonic flow will continue to promote surface pressure rises over\nthe intermountain West and dry N/NE boundary layer flow. The vast\nmajority of ensemble membership keeps any notable shortwave \nascent removed from the CWA, and confidence is very good that dry \nconditions will prevail through at least the middle of the week. \nWith H5 heights remaining nearly constant in a 580-584dm range, \nforecast numerical spread is rather narrow indicating very good \nconfidence in a persistence type forecast and readings very near \nclimatology. Ensemble spread grows markedly towards the end of \nnext week as pronounced northern stream pattern amplification \ndriven largely by an unusual early season sudden stratospheric \nwarming (SSW) event unfolds. The perturbation of the polar vortex \nshould cause poleward ridge retrogression while deep negative \nheight anomalies descend equatorward into the NE Conus and Pacific\nNW. While operational models are consistent in focusing strong \njet energy and Pacific moisture towards northern California with \ninconsequential shortwave energy into the Southwest, there is a \nnon- trivial amount of NAEFS members suggesting much deeper, \nintense troughing moving into the local area next weekend. Given \nthis amplification phase, continued wide ensemble spread and \npotentially large variations in operational models yielding low \nforecast confidence should be expected into next week. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1216Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nWinds will mostly favor an E to N direction through tonight with\nspeeds around 8 kts or less. However a period of NW winds may\ndevelop this evening, but confidence is low to moderate.\nAdditionally this evening, there is a low chance (10%) for VCSH,\nmainly between 01-08Z. There will be isolated showers and a few\nstorms in the high terrain to the north and east this afternoon\nthrough this evening. FEW to SCT cumulus around 6-7k ft AGL is\nexpected this morning through tonight.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo major aviation weather concerns are expected through the next\n24 hours under clear skies. Winds will be light through mid\nmorning with speeds aob 7 kt, with light and variable to calm\nwinds at times as well. By late morning wind speeds will pick up\nand be around 10-15 kt, with some gusts up around 20 kt expected\nduring the afternoon and evening. Wind directions will be out of\nthe west prior to going N/NW later this morning.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nA few isolated showers or thunderstorms may impact higher terrain \nareas north and east of Phoenix today, however wetting rainfall \nchances are minimal. Otherwise, dry conditions will prevail across \nthe region through at least the middle of next week. Minimum \nafternoon humidity levels will largely fall into a 20-30% range in \nwestern districts and 30-50% in eastern districts. This will follow \nfair to good overnight recovery in western districts and good to \nexcellent levels in eastern districts. Northerly winds gusts 20-30 \nmph will be common in western districts this afternoon through \nSaturday, while a period of enhanced morning easterly winds will \ndevelop over Arizona early next week. Outside of these time frames, \nwind speeds will be light with limited gustiness. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...18\nLONG TERM...18 \nAVIATION...Benedict/Berislavich\nFIRE WEATHER...18\n",
